WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:02.324
Picture one of your clients.

2
00:00:02.574 --> 00:00:04.151
She wants to buy a house.

3
00:00:04.951 --> 00:00:06.804
Now give her a staff of fifteen.

4
00:00:07.054 --> 00:00:09.999
A lawyer, a tax adviser, a pension
specialist,

5
00:00:10.039 --> 00:00:14.168
a negotiator, and an assistant who
remembers every promise.

6
00:00:14.539 --> 00:00:15.734
They never tire.

7
00:00:15.884 --> 00:00:17.149
They never forget.

8
00:00:17.299 --> 00:00:19.035
And they work for nobody else.

9
00:00:21.233 --> 00:00:23.050
A thought experiment for banks.

10
00:00:23.350 --> 00:00:27.264
What happens to the mortgage, when every
client arrives with that staff?

11
00:00:29.067 --> 00:00:32.026
One property. Twelve stages. Thirty years.

12
00:00:32.307 --> 00:00:36.275
From the first search, to the day it is
sold, or passed on to the heirs.

13
00:00:36.652 --> 00:00:40.666
Today, the bank hears from her at the
purchase, and again at maturity.

14
00:00:41.166 --> 00:00:45.492
With the staff, it hears from her at every
stage, for as long as she owns.

15
00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:49.470
Five past eight in the morning.

16
00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:52.609
One complete file goes out to 100 lenders.

17
00:00:52.729 --> 00:00:54.931
Banks, insurers, pension funds.

18
00:00:55.529 --> 00:00:56.752
You are one of them.

19
00:00:57.929 --> 00:01:01.559
Every quote carries the same date. Every
clause gets read.

20
00:01:03.567 --> 00:01:05.125
Now do the arithmetic.

21
00:01:05.375 --> 00:01:08.583
Today, a bank wins about one application
in four.

22
00:01:08.732 --> 00:01:12.545
Processing costs 6'000 francs for every
mortgage it wins.

23
00:01:13.138 --> 00:01:18.113
When agents ask 30 lenders, it is one in
forty. 60'000 francs.

24
00:01:18.689 --> 00:01:21.578
When the staff asks them all, it is one in
a hundred.

25
00:01:21.779 --> 00:01:26.017
150'000 francs, for a mortgage worth
20'000.

26
00:01:26.608 --> 00:01:32.760
To break even, an application may cost 200
francs. Today, it costs about 1'500.

27
00:01:34.967 --> 00:01:38.540
Most client processes rest on three quiet
assumptions.

28
00:01:38.825 --> 00:01:40.350
The client gets tired.

29
00:01:40.725 --> 00:01:42.043
The client forgets.

30
00:01:42.625 --> 00:01:44.832
And the bank is the expert in the room.

31
00:01:45.228 --> 00:01:47.670
The staff of fifteen removes all three.

32
00:01:49.767 --> 00:01:51.008
And when she sells?

33
00:01:51.309 --> 00:01:52.376
One listing.

34
00:01:52.526 --> 00:01:54.782
200 bidders, each with a staff.

35
00:01:54.932 --> 00:01:56.927
Each asking 100 lenders.

36
00:01:57.328 --> 00:02:00.536
20'000 financing checks, for a single
sale.

37
00:02:00.831 --> 00:02:04.028
Value the property once. Answer every
bidder from it.

38
00:02:06.133 --> 00:02:07.552
And here is the turn.

39
00:02:07.752 --> 00:02:11.173
The same staff is the best client a bank
has ever had.

40
00:02:11.573 --> 00:02:13.361
It delivers complete files.

41
00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:15.150
It answers within the hour.

42
00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:16.758
It reads what you send.

43
00:02:17.358 --> 00:02:18.892
It punishes one thing only.

44
00:02:18.932 --> 00:02:22.845
A process that works because the client
does not look closely.

45
00:02:25.233 --> 00:02:26.767
So what wins the tender?

46
00:02:27.067 --> 00:02:28.658
Being right the first time.

47
00:02:28.908 --> 00:02:30.377
Binding within hours.

48
00:02:30.624 --> 00:02:32.720
Documented as the work happens.

49
00:02:33.304 --> 00:02:37.839
Bots for the rules. Agents for the offer.
People for the moments that matter.

50
00:02:40.367 --> 00:02:41.778
Take one afternoon.

51
00:02:42.029 --> 00:02:44.343
Run it against your own mortgage process.

52
00:02:45.419 --> 00:02:48.409
The staff of fifteen may arrive later than
you think.

53
00:02:48.910 --> 00:02:50.480
It will not arrive tired.

